 are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=0$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=0\\cdot 1+0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=0-1+2=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=\\frac{1}{2}-1+2=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,z(1)≈\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535610 true] [17592186357413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357414 13194139535610 true] [17592186357413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357415 13194139535610 true] [17592186357413 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535610 true] [17592186357413 1041 "field-1" 13194139535610 true] [17592186357413 1104 #edb/ref 17592186357415 13194139535610 true] [17592186357413 1110 #uuid "7ab05ee9-a1c3-56f0-9405-ed79f9919aee" 13194139535610 true] [17592186357414 1034 "1" 13194139535610 true] [17592186357414 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535610 true] [17592186357414 1111 #uuid "523eb04f-e008-5aba-a683-b19269119ce1" 13194139535610 true] [17592186357415 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535610 true] [17592186357415 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535610 true] [17592186357415 1111 #uuid "bc358498-e85b-5902-9fa6-440aa6b4bd61" 13194139535610 true] [17592186357416 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357417 13194139535610 true] [17592186357416 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535610 true] [17592186357416 1041 "field-2" 13194139535610 true] [17592186357416 1104 #edb/ref 17592186357417 13194139535610 true] [17592186357416 1110 #uuid "8a83ff5a-d5c3-5f16-b715-cf1177a99fb0" 13194139535610 true] [17592186357417 1034 "\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139535610 true] [17592186357417 1103 #edb/ref 17